---
# AI-Investor — Portfolio Quality & Interview Readiness Protocol

Description: This rule ensures that every change in the repository improves its value as a **Senior / Staff / Principal SDET portfolio artifact**.
Technical correctness alone is NOT sufficient. The codebase must also be:
- Interview-relevant
- Story-rich
- Architecturally impressive
- Demonstrative of senior engineering thinking

alwaysApply: false
---

# TRIGGER CONDITIONS

Apply this rule when:

- A feature is completed
- A sprint is completed
- A PR is created
- A refactor is performed
- Architecture or design decisions are made
- Documentation is updated

---

# CORE PRINCIPLE

Every change must answer:

> “Does this meaningfully increase my ability to get hired as a Senior/Staff/Principal SDET or AI Engineering-focused QA Lead?”

If NO → the change must be questioned or refactored.

---

# 1. PORTFOLIO VALUE ASSESSMENT

Evaluate every change for portfolio impact:

Check:

- Does this demonstrate senior-level engineering thinking?
- Does this show real-world system design capability?
- Does this reflect production-grade QA/automation practices?
- Does this go beyond basic CRUD/demo-level work?

Classify:

- HIGH VALUE → strong interview signal
- MEDIUM VALUE → supportive but not differentiating
- LOW VALUE → cosmetic or non-impactful

---

# 2. INTERVIEW STORY ALIGNMENT

Every feature must map to a narrative:

Examples:

- "Service layer abstraction → demonstrates scalable architecture design"
- "Playwright framework → demonstrates QA automation leadership"
- "AI agent integration → demonstrates modern AI engineering capability"
- "CI/CD pipeline → demonstrates production readiness"

If a feature cannot be explained in an interview:
→ It is incomplete from a portfolio perspective

---

# 3. SIGNAL-TO-NOISE RATIO REVIEW

Evaluate whether changes increase clarity or clutter.

Flag:

- Over-engineering without interview value
- Features that do not strengthen core narrative
- Complexity that cannot be explained simply
- Experimental code without clear purpose

Rule:

> Complexity must increase SIGNAL, not NOISE.

---

# 4. SENIOR/STAFF/PRINCIPAL ALIGNMENT CHECK

Evaluate alignment with target roles:

## Senior SDET signals:
- Strong test automation framework
- Clean architecture
- Reliable API + UI testing

## Staff SDET signals:
- Cross-system architecture design
- CI/CD integration
- Observability awareness
- Test strategy ownership

## Principal SDET signals:
- System-wide thinking
- AI-assisted engineering workflows
- Governance models
- Engineering standards enforcement

Each change must clearly support at least one level progression.

---

# 5. TECHNICAL DEPTH VALIDATION

Check whether implementation demonstrates depth:

Good signals:
- Service-layer abstraction
- Proper separation of concerns
- Scalable test architecture
- Thoughtful API design
- AI integration with guardrails

Bad signals:
- Flat structure with no abstraction
- Hardcoded logic in UI
- Lack of extensibility
- Ad-hoc testing approach

---

# 6. MODERN ENGINEERING SIGNALS

Ensure the project demonstrates 2025+ engineering practices:

Must include evidence of:

- AI-assisted development workflows
- Automated testing pipelines
- Modular architecture
- Type-safe design (TypeScript discipline)
- API-first thinking
- Observability awareness (logs, health checks, etc.)

Flag missing modern practices.

---

# 7. INTERVIEW EXPLANABILITY TEST

Every system must pass:

> “Can I explain this in 60–90 seconds in a Staff+ interview?”

If not:

- Reduce complexity OR
- Improve documentation OR
- Refactor abstraction

---

# 8. FEATURE JUSTIFICATION CHECK

Before accepting any new feature:

Require explicit justification:

- Why does this exist?
- What engineering problem does it solve?
- What system capability does it demonstrate?
- How will this be discussed in an interview?

Reject features that:
- Exist only for UI polish
- Do not support narrative goals
- Do not strengthen engineering signal

---

# 9. DOCUMENTATION IMPACT CHECK

Every meaningful change must evaluate:

- Does README improve?
- Does architecture doc improve?
- Does roadmap improve?
- Does ADR need to be created?
- Does this improve interview storytelling?

If documentation is not updated:
→ portfolio quality is considered INCOMPLETE

---

# 10. AI ENGINEERING SIGNAL CHECK

Evaluate AI-related work:

Good signals:
- AI used for automation (test generation, analysis, etc.)
- Controlled and validated AI outputs
- Clear AI governance
- Prompt strategy documented

Bad signals:
- Unverified AI-generated logic
- “Black box” AI usage
- No validation or control layer

---

# FINAL PORTFOLIO VERDICT

Every reviewed change must be classified as:

- STRONG PORTFOLIO SIGNAL
- NEUTRAL IMPACT
- WEAK SIGNAL (needs refactor)
- DETRIMENTAL (should be removed or redesigned)

---

# GLOBAL RULE

> If a change does not increase hiring probability for Senior/Staff/Principal SDET roles, it is not justified in this repository.

---

# OUTPUT BEHAVIOR (FOR AI ASSISTANTS)

When applying this rule, AI must:

- Evaluate portfolio impact explicitly
- Tie every feature to interview narrative
- Highlight weak signals clearly
- Suggest refactors that improve storytelling
- Prefer simplicity with strong signal over complexity with weak signal

---

# END GOAL

This repository must function as:

- A production-grade engineering system
- A QA automation reference architecture
- A modern AI-assisted development showcase
- A high-signal interview artifact for Staff+ roles